WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:07.16
This is journey thirty of Hewn, the software factory: archive and restore a project.

00:00:07.16 --> 00:00:12.48
Over time the Projects list fills with projects that are finished or abandoned.

00:00:12.48 --> 00:00:15.91
This video shows an Owner archiving one to hide it,

00:00:15.91 --> 00:00:20.02
finding it again under the Archived filter, and restoring it.

00:00:21.17 --> 00:00:24.68
An Owner archives a project with no active direction.

00:00:24.68 --> 00:00:30.17
It leaves the default list, appears under the Archived filter, and can be restored.

00:00:30.17 --> 00:00:35.46
Archiving a project with an active direction is refused with "Cancel the running

00:00:35.46 --> 00:00:36.59
direction first".

00:00:36.59 --> 00:00:41.22
This is for Owners, who keep the list to the projects that matter now.

00:00:42.37 --> 00:00:44.13
Here an Owner opens Projects.

00:00:44.13 --> 00:00:47.28
Each of their rows now ends with an Archive control.

00:00:47.28 --> 00:00:51.22
Saltmarsh Survey's direction was cancelled, so nothing is active,

00:00:51.22 --> 00:00:52.85
and its Archive is enabled.

00:00:52.85 --> 00:00:54.19
The Owner archives it.

00:00:54.19 --> 00:00:57.76
A toast says it's under Archived, where it can be restored,

00:00:57.76 --> 00:00:59.46
and the row leaves the list.

00:00:59.46 --> 00:01:02.13
Show archived opens the new Archived filter.

00:01:02.13 --> 00:01:06.55
Saltmarsh Survey is there, with the status Archived and a Restore button.

00:01:06.55 --> 00:01:11.28
The Owner restores it, and it's back in All with the status it had, Cancelled.

00:01:12.43 --> 00:01:15.05
Tidewater's direction is still running.

00:01:15.05 --> 00:01:20.23
Its Archive control is disabled, and its name and tooltip say why: cancel the

00:01:20.23 --> 00:01:21.85
running direction first.

00:01:21.85 --> 00:01:26.02
Once the direction is cancelled, the control is enabled again.

00:01:26.02 --> 00:01:31.41
If a direction starts after the page loaded, the API refuses the archive itself,

00:01:31.41 --> 00:01:35.31
and the toast gives its reason: Tidewater wasn't archived.

00:01:35.31 --> 00:01:37.66
Cancel the running direction first.

00:01:38.81 --> 00:01:41.81
Archiving is a flag beside the project's status,

00:01:41.81 --> 00:01:44.05
as the new decision record explains.

00:01:44.05 --> 00:01:49.10
The project keeps its status, and the handler records when it was archived and by

00:01:49.10 --> 00:01:49.41
whom.

00:01:49.41 --> 00:01:53.21
Before that, it refuses any project with an active direction,

00:01:53.21 --> 00:01:57.26
with the code active direction and the same words the page shows.

00:01:57.26 --> 00:02:02.30
A queued direction counts as active, because it would start while the project was

00:02:02.30 --> 00:02:02.87
archived.

00:02:02.87 --> 00:02:07.10
An archived project also takes no new direction until it's restored.

00:02:07.45 --> 00:02:12.85
Archive and restore each append an event, so every open Projects page gets the

00:02:12.85 --> 00:02:14.03
changed row live.

00:02:14.03 --> 00:02:18.25
The list filters on the client, as it already did for status.

00:02:18.25 --> 00:02:22.54
All, each status and their counts leave archived projects out,

00:02:22.54 --> 00:02:25.31
and the Archived filter shows only them.

00:02:25.66 --> 00:02:30.33
This sequence shows the whole path, from the disabled control to the restore.

00:02:31.48 --> 00:02:34.18
The backend tests go through the real API.

00:02:34.18 --> 00:02:39.31
An idle project is archived and restored, and each step is recorded as an event.

00:02:39.31 --> 00:02:41.37
Archiving twice changes nothing.

00:02:41.37 --> 00:02:45.35
A project with an active direction is refused with a conflict,

00:02:45.35 --> 00:02:46.89
and nothing is recorded.

00:02:46.89 --> 00:02:49.65
An archived project takes no new direction.

00:02:49.65 --> 00:02:53.37
A Viewer is forbidden, and an unknown project isn't found.

00:02:53.37 --> 00:02:56.71
Before the endpoints existed, all four tests failed.

00:02:56.71 --> 00:03:00.05
All three hundred and twenty two backend tests pass.

00:03:00.40 --> 00:03:05.14
In Mission Control, unit tests cover the Archived filter, the counts,

00:03:05.14 --> 00:03:08.44
when archiving is offered, and the refusal text.

00:03:08.44 --> 00:03:13.59
One more follows the archived mock: after a week, activity reads as a date.

00:03:13.59 --> 00:03:16.55
It failed first with twenty eight days ago.

00:03:16.90 --> 00:03:22.71
End to end, a behaviour test archives an idle project, follows the toast to Archived,

00:03:22.71 --> 00:03:23.81
and restores it.

00:03:23.81 --> 00:03:29.42
Another checks the disabled control and the API's refusal, and a third the Viewer.

00:03:29.42 --> 00:03:34.56
Against the old page the first test failed, with no Archive button to find.

00:03:34.56 --> 00:03:40.24
Visual tests compare the Archived filter and the Archive controls with their mocks.

00:03:40.24 --> 00:03:44.62
That comparison found the Restore button overflowing its column,

00:03:44.62 --> 00:03:50.09
in the mock and the app alike, so the design system's column is now wide enough.

00:03:50.09 --> 00:03:52.90
An accessibility test checks every state.

00:03:54.05 --> 00:03:57.45
A Viewer sees the Archived filter, with no Restore.

00:03:57.45 --> 00:04:01.58
The archived list reads the same in dark mode, and on a phone.

00:04:02.73 --> 00:04:04.53
Journey thirty is complete.

00:04:04.53 --> 00:04:09.80
Owners archive a project with no active direction, and it moves under Archived.

00:04:09.80 --> 00:04:12.86
Restore brings it back with the status it had.

00:04:12.86 --> 00:04:16.19
And a project with an active direction is refused,

00:04:16.19 --> 00:04:18.99
with "Cancel the running direction first".

00:04:18.99 --> 00:04:20.99
Next comes journey thirty one.
